---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Avatar from '../components/Avatar.astro';
import { LINKEDIN_URL } from '../consts';
import dogs from '../assets/about/dogs.jpg';
import huayna from '../assets/about/huayna-picchu.jpg';
import blueHole from '../assets/about/blue-hole.jpg';
import waterfall from '../assets/about/waterfall.jpg';

const facts = [
  'Based in Indiana',
  '10+ years in Dynamics',
  'Loves to travel',
  'Dog dad of three',
  'Vegetarian',
  'Loves spicy food, especially salsa',
  'Michigan State alum',
];

const photos = [
  { src: dogs, alt: 'Two greyhounds and a whippet standing on a park path', caption: 'Geo, Max, and Gigi' },
  { src: huayna, alt: 'James and his wife with arms raised on the summit of Huayna Picchu, Machu Picchu below', caption: 'Top of Huayna Picchu, Peru' },
  { src: blueHole, alt: 'Aerial view of the Great Blue Hole from a small plane', caption: 'Flying over the Great Blue Hole, Belize' },
  { src: waterfall, alt: 'James and his wife in front of a waterfall in a mossy rock canyon in Iceland', caption: 'Chasing waterfalls in Iceland' },
];
---
<Base title="About" description="About James Torres: Dynamics 365 F&O architect, tinkerer, traveler, and dog dad.">
  <div class="container-read">
    <header class="about-head">
      <Avatar size={280} eager />
      <div>
        <h1>About me</h1>
        <p>Technical architect for Dynamics 365 F&amp;O, designing ERP solutions that fit how businesses actually run.</p>
      </div>
    </header>
    <ul class="quick-facts" aria-label="Quick facts">
      {facts.map((f) => <li>{f}</li>)}
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="container">
    <div class="photo-row">
      {photos.map((p) => (
        <figure>
          <Image src={p.src} alt={p.alt} widths={[400, 800]} sizes="(max-width: 700px) 50vw, 250px" />
          <figcaption>{p.caption}</figcaption>
        </figure>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="container-read">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Hi, I'm James</h2>
      <p>
        I've spent more than ten years in the Microsoft business applications world, most of it in
        Dynamics 365 Finance &amp; Operations. For years I've leaned on this community's blogs, forum answers, and conference sessions to get past my own
        hurdles.
      </p>
      <p>
        Speaking at Community Summit this year made something click: if something I write helps even one
        person get past a hurdle, or see a problem from a new angle, it's worth doing. This blog is my way
        of giving back to the community that built that foundation, and of joining the conversation instead
        of just reading it.
      </p>

      <h2>What you'll find here</h2>
      <p>
        Posts based on my own experiences and challenges. When I write a guide, I'll start with the scenario
        I was actually trying to solve, so you can tell whether it fits yours. Expect D365 F&amp;O development
        and architecture, practical uses of AI in ERP, and some thinking out loud about where headless ERP
        is going.
      </p>

      <h2>How I got here</h2>
      <p>
        I landed in Dynamics by accident. While hunting for internships, I ended up with two very different
        ones: the first at a software development shop, writing code all day, and the next in Dynamics
        consulting. Having seen both sides, the choice was easy. I still love building things, but consulting
        lets me use the skills I enjoy most: understanding how a business really runs, solving the problem
        behind the request, and shaping a solution around their secret sauce, the processes that make them
        who they are. That's what has kept me in this space ever since.
      </p>

      <h2>What I do</h2>
      <p>
        I'm a technical architect specializing in D365 F&amp;O, working across the connected Microsoft
        stack, including Power Platform and Azure. Most of my work is setting technical direction: shaping
        solution designs, making the trade-off calls, and guiding development teams from first workshop to
        go-live. I'm usually pulled in when a problem crosses boundaries: a performance issue spanning F&amp;O
        and the wider Microsoft ecosystem, an integration that has to write back, a development process that needs
        modernizing, or putting AI to practical use in X++ and configuration.
      </p>
      <p>More about my professional career on <a href={LINKEDIN_URL} rel="me noopener">LinkedIn</a>.</p>

      <h2>Building strong teams</h2>
      <p>
        Some of my favorite work has been growing the people around me: building an easier path for newer
        X++ developers to understand the stack, and giving them enough breadth to stand on their own two feet
        without having to figure everything out alone. Strong teams ship better solutions, and this blog is
        an extension of that.
      </p>

      <h2>Off the clock</h2>
      <p>
        I'm a natural tinkerer and all-around handyman. My wife and I travel whenever we can to explore the
        world and pick up new perspectives. At home I'm a proud dog dad to two greyhounds, Geo and Gigi, and
        a whippet named Max. I'm vegetarian and love spicy food, especially salsa. The hotter the better.
      </p>

      <div class="signoff">
        <p>
          Thanks for stopping by. If something here helps, or you'd approach it differently, come say hi on{' '}
          <a href={LINKEDIN_URL} rel="me noopener">LinkedIn</a>.
        </p>
        <p class="signature">Keep tinkering,<br />James</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .quick-facts { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0 0 32px; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; }
  .quick-facts li { font-size: 14px; padding: 5px 12px; border-radius: 999px; background: var(--accent-soft); color: var(--accent); font-weight: 500; }
  .photo-row { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: 16px; margin: 0 0 48px; }
  .photo-row figure { margin: 0; }
  .photo-row :global(img) { width: 100%; aspect-ratio: 4 / 5; object-fit: cover; border-radius: 12px; }
  .photo-row figcaption { font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--fg-subtle); margin-top: 8px; }
  @media (max-width: 700px) { .photo-row { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: 12px; } }
  .signoff { margin-top: 3em; padding-top: 1.5em; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .signature { font-style: italic; }
</style>
